
  <sn><md>### Ligand Exchange in Soil

**Definition:**
Ligand exchange is a **specific adsorption** process in which an anion (or ligand) in soil solution replaces a hydroxyl group (–OH) or water molecule (H₂O) attached to a metal ion (Fe³⁺, Al³⁺, Mn⁴⁺) on the surface of soil minerals, forming a **direct chemical bond** (inner-sphere complex).

**Mechanism:**

$$
\text{Fe} - \text{OH} + \text{H}_2\text{PO}_4^- \rightarrow \text{Fe} - \text{H}_2\text{PO}_4 + \text{OH}^-
$$

The phosphate ion replaces the –OH group and bonds directly with Fe³⁺ or Al³⁺. This is stronger than simple electrostatic (outer-sphere) attraction.

</md></sn>
